  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nBy Fri night tropical moisture will have moved inland across the \nregion from the Gulf, with a frontal boundary extending from the Mid-\nAtlantic states to SW TX.  Southerly flow across the area will \ncontinue to bring deep layered moisture into the state through the \nweekend as the frontal boundary pushes slowly southward. By Sat \nmorning the frontal boundary will have stalled just north of the \nstate and is forecast to stay there through Sun evening. This frontal \nboundary will act as a block not allowing the tropical moisture to \nmove out of the region while moisture continues to flow from the \nGulf in the southerly flow. This will put GA in a prime location to \nsee multiple inches of precipitation Fri through Mon. \n\nThe models have changed a bit with the location of the highest \namounts of precip as they are now showing higher amounts across \nSouth GA vs North GA. This go round seeing 2 to 4 inch totals across \ncentral GA with 1 to 3 inch totals across North GA. Due to the \nlimited amount of precipitation we have seen overt the past few \nweeks, central GA can handle a bit more rain than north GA but 2 to \n4 inches is still a lot of water in just 3 to 4 days. Will still \nneed to keep a close eye on the flooding potential across the area. \nStill not seeing much in the way of instability across the area but \nwe could still see some thunderstorms Fri and Sat. \n\nBy daybreak Monday the the frontal boundary will be moving south \nushering in a much drier and cooler airmass into North GA. This \nboundary does stall again across south GA/north FL so precip chances \nwill be relegated to central Georgia through day 7. Temps will be \nvery warm Fri with things beginning to cool as the precip moves in \nSat. High temp Fri expected in the 80s to lower 90s with highs Sat \nmainly in the 80s. High temps only expected to get up into the 70s \nSun through Wed.",
